@page "/not-found"

<PageTitle>No such page — DMARC Monitor</PageTitle>

@*
    Any URL that matches no route used to return 404 with a zero-length body:
    a blank white page, no layout, no navigation, no way back except the
    browser's back button. Mapped through UseStatusCodePagesWithReExecute in
    Program.cs so the status code is still a real 404 to anything reading it,
    while a person gets a page that looks like the rest of the application.
*@

<header class="page-head">
    <div>
        <h1>No such page</h1>
        <p class="sub">That address does not exist here.</p>
    </div>
</header>

<div class="empty">
    <h2>Where you probably meant to go</h2>
    <p>
        <a href="">Triage</a> — every domain, worst first. This is the front page.<br />
        <a href="domains">Domains</a> — one domain at a time, with its sending sources.<br />
        <a href="fix">Fix</a> — what would change in a customer's DNS.<br />
        <a href="import">Import</a> — drop in a folder or an archive of reports.
    </p>
    <p class="sub">A domain page is <code>/domains/&lt;domain&gt;</code>, so a typo in the
       domain name lands here rather than on the domain.</p>
</div>
